For ψ ∈ L²(ℝ), define its Fourier transform on L¹(ℝ) ∩ L²(ℝ) by ψ̂(ξ) = ∫_ℝ ψ(x)e^{−2πixξ} dx and extend to L²(ℝ). A function ψ ∈ L²(ℝ) is called an orthonormal wavelet if the system { 2^{j/2} ψ(2^j x − k) : j, k ∈ ℤ } is an orthonormal basis of L²(ℝ).

For an orthonormal wavelet ψ, define its wavelet dimension function D_ψ : ℝ → ℕ ∪ {0} by
   D_ψ(ξ) = Σ_{j=1}^{∞} Σ_{k∈ℤ} |ψ̂(2^j(ξ + k))|².

Let D : ℝ → ℕ ∪ {0} be any function satisfying the following four conditions:
 (D1) D is measurable and 1-periodic;
 (D2) D(ξ) + D(ξ + 1/2) = D(2ξ) + 1 for a.e. ξ ∈ ℝ;
 (D3) Σ_{k∈ℤ} 1_Δ(ξ + k) ≥ D(ξ) for a.e. ξ ∈ ℝ, where Δ = { ξ ∈ ℝ : D(2^{−j} ξ) ≥ 1 for all j ∈ ℕ ∪ {0} };
 (D4) liminf_{j→∞} D(2^{−j} ξ) ≥ 1 for a.e. ξ ∈ ℝ.

Question: Is the set
   { ψ ∈ L²(ℝ) : ψ is an orthonormal wavelet and D_ψ = D }
path-connected with respect to the L²(ℝ) norm?

